From 794abcf4708e4c1f8210ef690a84304992c90b1e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jason Perlow <jperlow@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 7 Sep 2026 01:00:21 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 24/24] fix(wallpaper-ocs-browser): dropdown tag filter,
 WallpaperCard parity, reliable thumbnails

Three operator-reported bugs on real hardware (.66), addressed in one
commit because they share a single UX problem: the OCS/Bing browser had
diverged from the main Desktop wallpaper picker's visual vocabulary and
from native-Singularity interaction patterns.

Bug 1: tag filter chips were not clickable
------------------------------------------
The previous tag row was a FlowBox of raw Singularity.Widgets.Chip
widgets. Singularity.Chip is a horizontal Box containing an internal
Button (which fires `activated` on click) plus a separate close-button.
Only the inner Button's hit area received clicks: clicking on the
chip's rounded-pill background -- the actual visible surface -- fell on
the Box, which has no click handler. The chip looked clickable, was
not. Category chips had the same wiring but were untested on hardware
so the bug surfaced only on the tag row first.

Bug 2: replace tag chip row with a dropdown
-------------------------------------------
Operator direction: replace the chip row with a SelectionRow dropdown.
libsingularity has no native multi-select dropdown widget, so the
tag axis falls back to single-select -- a tradeoff explicitly
documented in the comments around `rebuild_tag_row()` and the
`active_tag_ids` field. card_matches() still ANDs across text +
category + (single) tag, so the operator-facing contract
("narrow the grid to wallpapers carrying the tag I care about") is
preserved; what changes is that exactly one tag can be active at a
time instead of many. Category chips stay on raw Chips in a FlowBox
because categories are naturally single-select and ChipBar.set_active
works fine for them.

Bug 3: thumbnails showed as broken placeholders
----------------------------------------------
The Bing thumbnail path used `file.read_async` to open an InputStream
and then handed the open stream directly to
`Gdk.Pixbuf.from_stream_at_scale_async`. That loader assumes it owns
the stream and reads it synchronously to EOF; passing an already-open
stream can deadlock on the read loop and produces no pixbuf, leaving
the placeholder visible. The OCS Soup path worked but had the same
shape (drain to a ByteArray, then MemoryInputStream + loader). Fix:
drain the source stream to a ByteArray first, then build a
MemoryInputStream, then decode. Main-thread set_paintable() is
marshalled through Idle.add() because Picture.set_paintable is not
safe from arbitrary async contexts. Cancelled loads leave the
placeholder visible (matching the existing failure semantics) rather
than the prior behaviour, where cancelled loads in some paths could
leave stale pixbufs on the wrong card.

Visual parity with the main Desktop wallpaper picker
----------------------------------------------------
Reuses `WallpaperCard` (from desktop_page.vala) instead of duplicating
the box/overlay/picture/label chrome that add_card() used to hand-
build. Two small backward-compatible extensions on WallpaperCard:

  * `WallpaperCard.for_remote(uri, title, is_recent, loader)` --
    caller-supplied async loader for sources without a local file
    path.
  * `WallpaperCard.placeholder_only(uri, title)` -- chrome without
    any auto-load; the caller (the OCS browser's `thumbnails()` fan-
    out) drives set_paintable() itself for cancellable, generation-
    aware loads.
  * `set_action_button(Button)` -- overlays a caller-supplied button
    (Import / Pin) at the bottom-right, matching the recents-trash
    button's chrome.
  * `set_badge(string?)` -- small attribution/licence label reusing
    the wallpaper-card-title CSS class.

The existing `WallpaperCard(uri, is_recent)` constructor is unchanged;
all existing call sites continue to work. `internal` scope means the
class is already visible from wallpaper_ocs_browser.vala (same
compilation unit), so no visibility tweak was needed.

The OCS/Bing grid now uses the same .wallpaper-gallery / .wallpaper-
card / .workspace-preview CSS classes and the same 2-column Flow
structure as desktop_page.vala's wallpaper grid, so the visual
treatment is identical across both pickers.

Note on live-hardware reproducibility
-------------------------------------
Bug 1: I was unable to reproduce on hardware (no live Singularity
session available); the root cause is established by code reading of
Singularity.Widgets.Chip and confirmed structurally (the chip Box has
no click handler; only the inner Button does). The fix (replace with
a SelectionRow) is the operator's explicit direction regardless.

Bug 3: I was unable to reproduce on hardware either. The Bing path
fix is based on the documented Gdk.Pixbuf.from_stream_at_scale_async
contract (it owns the stream and reads it sync) and the fact that the
prior code passed it an already-open InputStream. Same caveat
applies.

Both bugs need real-hardware verification by the operator before
signing this off as completely fixed; the code is reviewed-and-
compiled-correct but a live GUI test was not possible from my end.
